Q: September&#39;s short form &quot;Sep&quot; no longer parses in Java 17 in en_GB locale A: <p>See Michael's answer: Unfortunately JDK16 introduced changed short form of September in en_GB locale from &quot;Sep&quot; to &quot;Sept&quot;. This causes issues both for generation and parsing of dates when using Locale.UK if your dates depend on format &quot;MMM&quot;.</p>
<p>Ideally, you'd fix all your data providers to use a sensible format such as <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ISO8601</a> so these type of locale changes do not affect you.</p>
<p>However, this is not always possible. Last time I checked, Bank Of England publishes data with annoying language specific format and has &quot;Sep&quot; not &quot;Sept&quot; so you can't use default UK locale of newer JDK to parse those dates. Also you may be running various server installations which a mix of JDK11 through latest and interoperability of old/new systems, and you may have need to backport new code to older installations.</p>
<p><strong>Workarounds if you have dates containing &quot;Sep&quot;</strong></p>
<p>Specify US locale for those date formats using &quot;MMM&quot;, and works pre+post JDK16:</p>
<pre><code>String dateStr = &quot;20-Sep-2012&quot;;
DateTimeFormatter format = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(&quot;dd-MMM-yyyy&quot;, Locale.US);
System.out.format(&quot;parse %s =&gt; %s %n&quot;, dateStr, LocalDate.parse(dateStr, format));
</code></pre>
<p>Or, keep with Locale.UK and just search replace. Note that this code is not portable to older JDK deployments:</p>
<pre><code>String dateStr = &quot;20-Sep-2012&quot;;
DateTimeFormatter format = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(&quot;dd-MMM-yyyy&quot;);
dateStr = dateStr.replace(&quot;Sep&quot;, &quot;Sept&quot;);
System.out.format(&quot;parse %s =&gt; %s %n&quot;, dateStr, LocalDate.parse(dateStr, format));
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Use DateTimeFormatterBuilder</strong></p>
<p>Use your own <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/time/format/DateTimeFormatterBuilder.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DateTimeFormatterBuilder</a> with alternative handlers to set sets month 9 as Sep AND Sept in optional sections. This code is portable to all JDK11+ versions, and runs with US or  UK locale for parsing both &quot;Sep&quot; and &quot;Sept&quot;.</p>
<pre><code>for (var locale : List.of(Locale.UK, Locale.US)) {
    DateTimeFormatter formatter = new DateTimeFormatterBuilder()
    .parseCaseInsensitive()
    .appendPattern(&quot;dd&quot;)
    .appendLiteral('-')
    // Handles Sept as month 9:
    .optionalStart()
    .appendText(ChronoField.MONTH_OF_YEAR, Map.of(9L, &quot;Sept&quot;))
    .optionalEnd()
    // Accepts default MMM logic:
    .optionalStart()
    .appendPattern(&quot;MMM&quot;)
    .optionalEnd()
    // Handles Sep as month 9:
    .optionalStart()
    .appendText(ChronoField.MONTH_OF_YEAR, Map.of(9L, &quot;Sep&quot;))
    .optionalEnd()
    .appendLiteral('-')
    .appendPattern(&quot;yyyy&quot;)
    .toFormatter(locale);
    System.out.format(&quot;%nformatter: %s%n%n&quot;, formatter);

    for (var s : List.of(
            &quot;10-Jan-2020&quot;, &quot;11-Feb-2021&quot;, &quot;12-Mar-2022&quot;, &quot;13-Apr-2023&quot;
            , &quot;14-May-2024&quot;, &quot;15-Jun-2025&quot;, &quot;16-Jul-2026&quot;, &quot;17-Aug-2027&quot;
            , &quot;18-Sep-2029&quot;, &quot;19-Oct-2030&quot;, &quot;20-Nov-2031&quot;, &quot;21-Dec-2032&quot;
            , &quot;17-Sept-2028&quot;)) {

        try {
            LocalDate ldt = LocalDate.parse(s, formatter);
            System.out.format(&quot;local: %s parse %s =&gt; %s %n&quot;, locale, s, ldt);

        } catch(DateTimeParseException failed) {
            System.out.format(&quot;local: %s parse %s FAILED %s%n&quot;, locale, s, failed);
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Prints same in JDK11 and JDK25:</p>
<pre><code>formatter: ParseCaseSensitive(false)Value(DayOfMonth,2)'-'[Text(MonthOfYear)][Text(MonthOfYear,SHORT)][Text(MonthOfYear)]'-'Value(YearOfEra,4,19,EXCEEDS_PAD)

local: en_GB parse 10-Jan-2020 =&gt; 2020-01-10
local: en_GB parse 11-Feb-2021 =&gt; 2021-02-11
local: en_GB parse 12-Mar-2022 =&gt; 2022-03-12
local: en_GB parse 13-Apr-2023 =&gt; 2023-04-13
local: en_GB parse 14-May-2024 =&gt; 2024-05-14
local: en_GB parse 15-Jun-2025 =&gt; 2025-06-15
local: en_GB parse 16-Jul-2026 =&gt; 2026-07-16
local: en_GB parse 17-Aug-2027 =&gt; 2027-08-17
local: en_GB parse 18-Sep-2029 =&gt; 2029-09-18
local: en_GB parse 19-Oct-2030 =&gt; 2030-10-19
local: en_GB parse 20-Nov-2031 =&gt; 2031-11-20
local: en_GB parse 21-Dec-2032 =&gt; 2032-12-21
local: en_GB parse 17-Sept-2028 =&gt; 2028-09-17

formatter: ParseCaseSensitive(false)Value(DayOfMonth,2)'-'[Text(MonthOfYear)][Text(MonthOfYear,SHORT)][Text(MonthOfYear)]'-'Value(YearOfEra,4,19,EXCEEDS_PAD)

local: en_US parse 10-Jan-2020 =&gt; 2020-01-10
local: en_US parse 11-Feb-2021 =&gt; 2021-02-11
local: en_US parse 12-Mar-2022 =&gt; 2022-03-12
local: en_US parse 13-Apr-2023 =&gt; 2023-04-13
local: en_US parse 14-May-2024 =&gt; 2024-05-14
local: en_US parse 15-Jun-2025 =&gt; 2025-06-15
local: en_US parse 16-Jul-2026 =&gt; 2026-07-16
local: en_US parse 17-Aug-2027 =&gt; 2027-08-17
local: en_US parse 18-Sep-2029 =&gt; 2029-09-18
local: en_US parse 19-Oct-2030 =&gt; 2030-10-19
local: en_US parse 20-Nov-2031 =&gt; 2031-11-20
local: en_US parse 21-Dec-2032 =&gt; 2032-12-21
local: en_US parse 17-Sept-2028 =&gt; 2028-09-17
</code></pre>
<p>See also <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/77656370/date-time-formatter-use-same-pattern-but-different-output-with-similar-date-stri">Date Time Formatter use same pattern but different output with similar date string format</a></p>

Q: How do I call &quot;cpuid&quot; in Linux? A: <p>With GCC there is no need to invoke <code>cpuid</code> from inline assembler,
because it provides the <code>__get_cpuid_count()</code> and <code>__get_cpuid()</code> <a href="https://github.com/gcc-mirror/gcc/blob/8758503918a91dacff4dbc7126eced21787fbfc9/gcc/config/i386/cpuid.h#L323-L358" rel="nofollow noreferrer">intrinsics via <code>cpuid.h</code></a>.</p>
<p><code>__get_cpuid_count()</code> is more generic as it also supports specifying a cpuid sub-leaf (i.e. ECX) as input parameter, whereas <code>__get_cpuid()</code> only is usable for CPUID leaves that don't have any sub-leaves.</p>
<p>Example that queries CPUID.07h.0:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;cpuid.h&gt;

#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;


int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
    unsigned eax = 0;
    unsigned ebx = 0;
    unsigned ecx = 0;
    unsigned edx = 0;

    r = __get_cpuid_count(7, 0, &amp;eax, &amp;ebx, &amp;ecx, &amp;edx);
    if (!r) {
        fprintf(stderr, &quot;cpuid leaf 7 unsupported ...\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }
    printf(&quot;cpuid.07h.0: eax=0x%x ebx=0x%x ecx=0x%x edx=0x%x\n&quot;,
            r, eax, ebx, ecx, edx);
}

</code></pre>

Q: How to call a method with generic parameter of a self referencing generic interface? A: <p>Your declaration</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public interface ISelfReferenceInterface&lt;S extends ISelfReferenceInterface&lt;S&gt;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>is obviously intended to be implemented by classes having itself as argument for <code>S</code>. But it doesn’t enforce it. In facts, it’s impossible to enforce at a type declaration at all.</p>
<p>But you can enforce this relationship in generic code</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public static &lt;I extends ISelfReferenceInterface&lt;I&gt;&gt;
              void genericMethod(I someInterfaceImplementation)
{
  someInterfaceImplementation.genericMethod(someInterfaceImplementation);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This shows, how the constraint can be enforced and the method can be invoked without knowing the actual implementation class.</p>
<p>But note that with the declaration</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>ISelfReferenceInterface&lt;SelfReferenceClass&gt; argInterface = new SelfReferenceClass();
</code></pre>
<p>you are losing the information that the implementation fulfills this constraint. As shown in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79897329/2711488">this answer</a>, you can have other implementations of the interface violating the contract (and as said above, you can’t prevent them). And such implementations can be assigned to that variable, i.e.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>class ViolatingYourIntention implements ISelfReferenceInterface&lt;SelfReferenceClass&gt;
{
  @Override
  public void genericMethod(SelfReferenceClass obj)
  {
  }
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>ISelfReferenceInterface&lt;SelfReferenceClass&gt; argInterface
                                            = new ViolatingYourIntention();
</code></pre>
<p>So the compiler is right in rejecting attempts to pass <code>argInterface</code> to <code>genericMethod</code>.</p>
<p>So your code should either, work with the implementation class, which includes all code that has to create instances of it directly, i.e. without a generic factory, or be entirely generic like the <code>genericMethod</code>.</p>
<p>There are existing examples of this pattern. Nothing can prevent you from creating an oddball implementation of <code>Comparable</code>, not being comparable to itself. But you can’t pass a list of such implementations to generic methods like <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Collections.html#sort(java.util.List)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Collections.sort(…)</code></a>.</p>
<p>So, you never write <code>Comparable&lt;String&gt;</code> anywhere. You either work with <code>String</code> directly or pass the strings to entirely generic methods, like <code>sort</code>, which do not refer to <code>String</code> at all.</p>
